  "gap_note": {
    "question": "Does the gap between the attack and the bound come from same-label gradient similarity?",
    "answer": "Yes for same-label candidates at u 10 and 100, mostly no for any-label candidates at u 10. Same-label candidates have clipped gradients that point the same way as the victim's (mean cosine about 0.8, against about 0.01 for any-label candidates). Their exact optimum sits far below the orthogonal-candidate reference at low and medium noise (same-label u 10: 0.32 below at sigma 0.2, 0.38 at sigma 0.5), while for any-label u 10 it is within 0.07 of that reference at every sigma. At sigma 4 every gap is small. The same-label u 10 gap of 0.055 splits into 0.007 for the bound itself, 0.026 for candidate similarity and 0.022 for sampling noise in 1000 trials. For any-label u 100 and 1000 at low noise, a share of the gap comes from candidates of the victim's own class that sit close to the victim by chance (nearest other candidate 0.44 C at u 100 and 0.31 C at u 1000), and from the bound itself being loose for large u. All clipped gradients have norm C here (every per-sample gradient exceeds the clip), so the inner-product and nearest-candidate rules choose the same candidate in every trial.",
